\description{
A model-based management procedure that fits a surplus production model to
the catch and one or more relative abundance indices with \code{\link[=FitSP]{FitSP()}}, sets a
fishing mortality from the estimated stock status with a harvest control
rule, and projects the fitted model to calculate the TAC (or effort) for
the next management interval.
}
\details{
\subsection{Model parameters}{

The leading parameters of the surplus production model are:
\itemize{
\item \code{FMSY}: the fishing mortality that produces MSY. Always estimated.
\item \code{MSY}: the maximum sustainable yield, in catch units. Always estimated.
\item \code{Depletion}: biomass at the start of the first fitted year relative to
\code{K}. Fixed at \code{Depletion} (default \code{1}, an unfished stock at the start
of the fitted years), or estimated with \code{EstDepletion = TRUE}.
\item \code{Shape}: the shape parameter \code{n} of the production function, which sets
\code{BMSY/K = n^(1/(1-n))}. \code{n = 2} (the default) is the Schaefer model
(\code{BMSY/K = 0.5}), and \code{n = 1} the Fox model (\code{BMSY/K = exp(-1)}, the
limit as \code{n} approaches \code{1}). Fixed at \code{Shape}, or estimated with
\code{EstShape = TRUE} with a lognormal prior (median \code{Shape}, CV \code{ShapeCV},
or \code{Priors$Shape}).
}

The derived quantities are \code{BMSY = MSY / FMSY} and \code{K = BMSY / (BMSY/K)}.
Lognormal priors can be set on any estimated leading parameter with
\code{Priors}; by default \code{FMSY} has a vague prior (median \code{0.2}, CV \code{2}). With \code{Model = 'internal'}, the catchability of each index is
calculated at its maximum likelihood value given the leading parameters,
and the observation standard deviation of each index is either calculated
the same way (\code{IndexSD = 'estimate'}) or set from the index \code{CV}
(\code{IndexSD = 'data'}). See \code{\link[=FitSP]{FitSP()}} for the model equations, and
\code{\link[=SpictControl]{SpictControl()}} for how the leading parameters map to the \code{spict}
parameterisation with \code{Model = 'spict'}, which also estimates process
error and catch observation error.
}

\subsection{Procedure}{
\enumerate{
\item The model is fitted to the calendar-year data (see \code{\link[=FitSP]{FitSP()}} for the
models and the index selection arguments, and \code{\link[=AnnualData]{AnnualData()}} for
seasonal data). Each fit starts from the estimates of the previous
management cycle, if these were made with the same \code{Model} (\code{WarmStart}
in \code{\link[=SPControl]{SPControl()}}/\code{\link[=SpictControl]{SpictControl()}}).
\item Biomass at the start of \code{AdviceYear} is calculated by projecting the
fitted model through any years between the last fitted year and
\code{AdviceYear} (when there is a data lag, or \code{FitYears} ends before the
last data year), with the catch set by \code{GapCatch}.
\item The fishing mortality is
\code{HockeyStickHCR(tunepar * FTarget * FMSY, Est = B/BMSY, Ref = 1)}, where
\code{B/BMSY} is the projected status at the start of \code{AdviceYear} and the
control points are \code{HCRControlPointsBiomass}/\code{HCRControlPointsRate} (see
\code{\link[=HockeyStickHCR]{HockeyStickHCR()}}). With \code{FractileB} (\code{FractileF}), the \code{FractileB}
(\code{FractileF}) quantile of \code{B/BMSY} (\code{FMSY}) is used instead of the point
estimate, assuming a lognormal distribution with the standard error of
the estimate (for \code{B/BMSY}, the standard error at the start of the year
after the last fitted year).
\item The model is projected at that fishing mortality for the \code{Interval}
years of the management cycle. The TAC is the mean (\code{TACYears = 'mean'})
or first-year (\code{'first'}) catch. With \code{AdviceType = 'Effort'}, the advice
is the effort relative to the last historical year, \code{F / F(YearLH)}.
\item The TAC is converted to \code{TACType} using the landed fraction of the
removals over the last \code{LandedFracYears} data years (when \code{TACType}
differs from \code{CatchType}). The change from the previous TAC (or effort)
is scaled by \code{Responsiveness} and constrained by \code{DeltaDown} and
\code{DeltaUp}, and the TAC is bounded by \code{TACRange} (see \code{\link[=ConstrainTAC]{ConstrainTAC()}}).
If the previous TAC is zero, the TAC is only bounded by \code{TACRange}.
}

If the data cannot be prepared, fewer than \code{MinIndexYears} years have
index observations, the model fit fails or does not converge, the
standard errors needed by \code{FractileB}/\code{FractileF} are not available, or
(with \code{AdviceType = 'Effort'}) the estimated fishing mortality in the last
historical year is zero, the advice is set by \code{OnFail}: \code{'hold'} keeps
the previous TAC (or effort), and \code{'trend'} changes it by the ratio of the
mean index over the last \code{FallbackYears[1]} fitted years to the mean over
the \code{FallbackYears[2]} years before them (the geometric mean over indices,
weighted by \code{IndexWeight}, and raised to \code{Responsiveness}). If the data
cannot be prepared, \code{'trend'} keeps the previous advice. The fallback
advice is constrained as in
step 5. Each such management cycle is recorded as a warning in the \code{Log}
of the returned \link[=advice-class]{advice} object.
}

\subsection{Diagnostics}{

The estimates of each management cycle are stored in the \code{Misc} slot of
the returned \link[=advice-class]{advice} object, as the list
\code{Advice@Misc$SurplusProduction}. Its contents are set by the
\code{Diagnostics} argument:
\itemize{
\item \code{Model} and \code{par}: the model and the parameter estimates, used as
starting values for the next fit. Always stored.
\item \code{Summary} (\code{'min'} and \code{'full'}): a one-row \code{data.frame} with
\code{AdviceYear}, \code{LastDataYear}, \code{Converged}, \code{Fallback}, the estimated
\code{B_BMSY} and \code{F_FMSY}, \code{BAdvice_BMSY} (the projected status at the start
of \code{AdviceYear}), \code{MSY}, \code{FMSY}, \code{FAdvice} (the fishing mortality from
the harvest control rule), \code{Advice} (the TAC or relative effort before
allocation), and \code{Message} (\code{'Converged'}, or the reason the advice was
set by \code{OnFail}).
\item \code{Indices} (\code{'min'} and \code{'full'}): a \code{data.frame} with one row per index
used in the fit: \code{Index}, \code{Source} (\code{'Survey'} or \code{'CPUE'}), \code{Units},
\code{Timing}, \code{Weight} (the normalised \code{IndexWeight}), \code{nObs} (number of
positive observations in the fitted years), and the estimated \code{q} and
\code{Sigma} (\code{NA} when the advice is set by \code{OnFail}). The relative weight of
each index in the fit is \code{Weight / Sigma^2}.
\item \code{Fit} (\code{'full'} only): the \code{spfit} object returned by \code{\link[=FitSP]{FitSP()}},
including the estimated biomass and fishing mortality time series, the
index catchabilities and standard deviations, the standard errors, and
the \code{Log} of the fit, without the \code{Prep} and \code{Spict} elements. Not
stored when the advice is set by \code{OnFail}.
}

In a projection, the advice objects are kept in \code{MSE@Misc$Advice},
indexed by MP, management year, simulation, and stock (complex).
\code{\link[=SPEstimates]{SPEstimates()}} combines the \code{Summary} of every management cycle into one
\code{data.frame} and adds the operating model values of \code{B/BMSY} and
\code{F/FMSY} (from \code{\link[=B_BMSY]{B_BMSY()}} and \code{\link[=F_FMSY]{F_FMSY()}}) for the same years, to compare
the estimated and true stock status. \code{SPEstimates(Type = 'Indices')}
combines the \code{Indices} of every management cycle. The fit for a single data set can be
examined directly with \code{\link[=FitSP]{FitSP()}}, using the same arguments.

Each management cycle with advice set by \code{OnFail} is also recorded as a
\code{SurplusProduction} warning in \code{MSE@Log}, with the reason the fit was not
used; \code{Log(MSE, type = 'warning')} lists these by MP, year, and
simulation. The \code{Converged} and \code{Fallback} columns of \code{\link[=SPEstimates]{SPEstimates()}}
give the same information as a \code{data.frame}.
}

\subsection{Tuning}{

\code{tunepar = 1} applies the MP as specified; larger values increase the
target fishing mortality proportionally. See \code{\link[=TuneMP]{TuneMP()}}.
}
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
Hist <- Simulate(SingleStockOM)
SurplusProduction(Hist@Data[[1]][[1]])

SP_Fox <- SetMPArgs(SurplusProduction, Shape = 1, FTarget = 0.8)
MSE <- Project(Hist, MPs = list(SP = SurplusProduction, SP_Fox = SP_Fox))
SPEstimates(MSE)
}

}
